<What changed and why, in a few sentences.>

Verification: <the just recipes or targeted tests run, and anything not run.>

# Applies to development commits, PR descriptions and squash messages.
# Read and follow this standard even when supplying a message via CLI or API.
# Type: feat, fix, refactor, test, docs or chore.
# Subject: imperative, specific, at most 72 characters.
# Body: concise factual prose, wrapped at 72 characters. Describe the
# concrete change and its reason for a reader without session context.
# Cite the relevant architecture section or ADR for architectural changes.
# Verification: state actual checks and results, failures and unverified
# guarantees. Distinguish local execution from CI and reported evidence.
# Exclude metaphors, promotional claims, personal or conversational details,
# agent attribution, descriptions of effort and development-session logs.
# Do not use em dashes or claim more than the evidence establishes.
# Squash: write one message describing the complete reviewed outcome.
# Do not concatenate development messages or recount abandoned approaches.
